<item>
            <title>[LU-20664] rustreapi: lsp_pool is set from an unterminated Rust String</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20664</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;rustreapi passes an unterminated Rust string to liblustreapi as the pool name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenOptions::get_stripe_param() (rustreapi/rustreapi/src/lib.rs:229) fills&lt;br/&gt;
llapi_stripe_param.lsp_pool from the bytes of a Rust String:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    let pool = if let Some(pool) = self.pool.as_ref() &lt;/p&gt;
{
            pool.as_bytes().as_ptr() as *mut c_char
    }
&lt;p&gt; else &lt;/p&gt;
{
            ptr::null_mut::&amp;lt;c_char&amp;gt;()
    }
&lt;p&gt;;&lt;br/&gt;
    ...&lt;br/&gt;
    addr_of_mut!((*param).lsp_pool).write(pool);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;`pool` is Option&amp;lt;String&amp;gt; (line 97). A Rust String stores exactly its bytes with&lt;br/&gt;
no trailing NUL, but liblustreapi treats lsp_pool as a C string:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul class=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;liblustreapi.c:478   const char *pool_name = param-&amp;gt;lsp_pool;&lt;br/&gt;
                         then lov_pool_is_ignored(pool_name)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;llapi_stripe_param_verify(), which strlen()s it against LOV_MAXPOOLNAME&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;liblustreapi.c:881   snprintf(lmu-&amp;gt;lum_pool_name, sizeof(...), &quot;%s&quot;,&lt;br/&gt;
                                  param-&amp;gt;lsp_pool)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Each of those reads past the end of the String&apos;s heap allocation until it&lt;br/&gt;
happens to find a zero byte. Depending on what follows in memory the effects&lt;br/&gt;
are: garbage appended to the pool name so a valid pool fails with EINVAL or&lt;br/&gt;
ENOENT, a length that trips the LOV_MAXPOOLNAME check, or a heap over-read&lt;br/&gt;
fault when no zero byte precedes the end of the mapping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reproducer:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    OpenOptions::new().pool(Some(&quot;mypool&quot;.to_string())).open(path)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Note this is not a dangling pointer: self.pool is owned by the OpenOptions,&lt;br/&gt;
which outlives the llapi_* call, so the memory is valid. Only the terminator&lt;br/&gt;
is missing. That shapes the fix &amp;#8211; a CString built inside get_stripe_param()&lt;br/&gt;
would be dropped at return and &lt;b&gt;would&lt;/b&gt; dangle, because open() calls&lt;br/&gt;
assume_init() and then passes &amp;amp;param to llapi_file_open_param() after&lt;br/&gt;
get_stripe_param() has returned. The terminator has to be owned by something&lt;br/&gt;
that outlives the call; storing pool as a CString rather than a String is the&lt;br/&gt;
straightforward option, since self already has the right lifetime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same class of bug in OpenOptions::volatile(), which passed&lt;br/&gt;
dir.as_os_str().as_encoded_bytes().as_ptr() as the directory argument, is&lt;br/&gt;
fixed in &lt;a href=&quot;https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/58514&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/c/fs/lustre-release/+/58514&lt;/a&gt; (&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18842&quot; title=&quot;lustreapi: add a volatile function that supports layouts&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-18842&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-18842&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
